-- so the peak count is strictly below the class count, on a shift assignment proved to respect the
-- symmetry. This is accidental equivalence, and it is a different thing from being one class: the two
-- beta protons of leucine are two classes that happen to resonate together. The consequence runs the
-- other way from the valine case. There, the bond graph predicted too few classes and the spectrum
-- showed the true number. Here, the configured molecule predicts the true number of classes and the
-- spectrum shows fewer peaks than there are classes. A count read off a spectrum is therefore a lower
-- bound on the classes and not the classes, and any procedure that reads structural equivalence off
-- observed equivalence -- which is what automatic assignment does -- infers a symmetry the molecule does
-- not have.
lu-peaks-below-classes : peak-count Leu lu-shift lu-h ≡ class-count Leu lu-eq lu-rank lu-conf-gens 22 lu-h → Empty
lu-peaks-below-classes q = seven-not-eight (concat (sym lu-peaks) (concat q lu-signals-conf))
